title: Resolving paths
tags: [[Reconcile]]
kind: guide
summary: mf path prints where a metarecord's file is — absolute, or relative to the repository — and --resolve-tree does it for a whole selection.

```
mf -n music path 3f2a…                # /data/music/jazz/blue.flac
mf -n music path 3f2a… --relative     # /jazz/blue.flac ("/" for the root)
mf -n music metarecord -q 'tag -> (path = "jazz")' get --resolve-tree mfr_path
```

`mf path` resolves one metarecord's `mfr_path` to its absolute path (the repository's
root from `GET /repos`, joined with the position's path) in one request; `--relative`
prints the repository-relative form, with a leading `/` and the root as `/`. It fails
(exit code 1) when the metarecord has no position — untracked, or deleted.

For many metarecords at once, `mf metarecord … get --resolve-tree <field>` resolves any
`tree_ref` field of the selection in one request
([[Editing metarecords from the command line]]). The conventions of those paths:
[[TreeRef path conventions]].
